### What is the internal structure of ArrayList?

At its core, ArrayList is an `Object[]` array with a `size` counter. The array holds element references, and `size` tracks how many of those slots are actually used.

Here is a simplified version of the key fields and methods from the OpenJDK source:

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class SimpleArrayList<E> {
    private static final int DEFAULT_CAPACITY = 10;
    private Object[] elementData;
    private int size;

    // Default constructor: lazy initialization
    public SimpleArrayList() {
        this.elementData = new Object[0]; // empty until first add
    }

    // Capacity constructor: pre-allocate
    public SimpleArrayList(int initialCapacity) {
        this.elementData = new Object[initialCapacity];
    }

    // add(E e): append at end
    public boolean add(E e) {
        if (size == elementData.length) {
            grow();
        }
        elementData[size++] = e;
        return true;
    }

    // get(int index): O(1) direct access
    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public E get(int index) {
        if (index >= size) throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("Index: " + index);
        return (E) elementData[index];
    }

    // grow: create new array of 1.5x capacity
    private void grow() {
        int oldCapacity = elementData.length;
        int newCapacity = (oldCapacity == 0)
            ? DEFAULT_CAPACITY
            : oldCapacity + (oldCapacity >> 1); // 1.5x
        elementData = Arrays.copyOf(elementData, newCapacity);
    }

    public int size() { return size; }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SimpleArrayList<String> accounts = new SimpleArrayList<>();
        accounts.add("SAV2001");
        accounts.add("CUR3001");
        accounts.add("FD4001");
        System.out.println("Element at 1: " + accounts.get(1));
        System.out.println("Size: " + accounts.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Element at 1: CUR3001
Size: 3
```

### How does the growth formula work?

The formula is `newCapacity = oldCapacity + (oldCapacity >> 1)`. The `>> 1` is a bitwise right shift, which divides by 2. So the formula is `old + old/2 = 1.5 * old`.

The growth sequence starting from the default capacity of 10:

| Resize number | Old capacity | New capacity | Elements copied |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 10 | 15 | 10 |
| 2 | 15 | 22 | 15 |
| 3 | 22 | 33 | 22 |
| 4 | 33 | 49 | 33 |
| 5 | 49 | 73 | 49 |
| 6 | 73 | 109 | 73 |
| 7 | 109 | 163 | 109 |
| 8 | 163 | 244 | 163 |
| 9 | 244 | 366 | 244 |
| 10 | 366 | 549 | 366 |

To reach 1 million elements starting from capacity 10, ArrayList resizes about 40 times, copying all elements each time. That is why pre-allocation matters.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class GrowthSequenceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int capacity = 10;
        int resizeCount = 0;
        int targetSize = 1_000_000;

        System.out.println("Resize | Old Cap | New Cap");
        while (capacity < targetSize) {
            int oldCapacity = capacity;
            capacity = capacity + (capacity >> 1);
            resizeCount++;
            if (resizeCount <= 15) { // show first 15
                System.out.printf("%6d | %7d | %7d%n", resizeCount, oldCapacity, capacity);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("...");
        System.out.println("Total resizes to reach " + targetSize + ": " + resizeCount);
    }
}
```

