### Why is the growth factor 1.5x and not 2x?

Java's ArrayList grows by 1.5x. C++ `std::vector` also typically grows by 1.5x (MSVC, libc++). Some implementations (GCC libstdc++) grow by 2x.

The argument for 1.5x over 2x is about memory reuse. When the growth factor is 2x, the new array is always larger than the sum of all previously discarded arrays, so the JVM can never reuse the old memory. With 1.5x, after several rounds of growth, the new array can fit into the space left by previous discarded arrays.

In practice, the difference is small for most applications. But for a banking system loading millions of records, 1.5x growth wastes less memory than 2x growth.

For comparison, `Vector` (the legacy class) grows by 2x: 10 to 20 to 40 to 80 to 160.

### How do add and remove work internally?

Adding at the end is simple: place the element at `elementData[size]` and increment `size`. If the array is full, grow first. This is amortized O(1) because resizing happens infrequently.

Adding in the middle requires shifting all subsequent elements one position to the right using `System.arraycopy()`. This is O(n) because every element after the insertion point must move.

Removing works the same way in reverse: shift all subsequent elements one position to the left, then null out the last slot (to help garbage collection).

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class AddRemoveInternalsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("A", "B", "C", "D", "E"));
        System.out.println("Original: " + accounts);

        // add(2, "X") – insert at index 2
        // Internally: shift C, D, E right by 1, then set index 2 to X
        accounts.add(2, "X");
        System.out.println("After add(2, X): " + accounts);
        // [A, B, X, C, D, E] – C, D, E all shifted right

        // remove(2) – remove index 2
        // Internally: shift C, D, E left by 1, null out last slot
        accounts.remove(2);
        System.out.println("After remove(2): " + accounts);
        // [A, B, C, D, E] – C, D, E shifted back left

        // add at end – no shifting needed
        accounts.add("F");
        System.out.println("After add(F): " + accounts);
    }
}
```

```text
Original: [A, B, C, D, E]
After add(2, X): [A, B, X, C, D, E]
After remove(2): [A, B, C, D, E]
After add(F): [A, B, C, D, E, F]
```

### How do you set initial capacity correctly?

When you know (or can estimate) the final number of elements, pass the expected size to the constructor. This avoids repeated resize-and-copy cycles.

The rule of thumb: if you are loading N elements, use `new ArrayList<>(N)`. If you are loading elements from a query where the count is unknown but you can estimate an upper bound, use that estimate.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class BulkLoadDemo {
    record Account(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int expectedCount = 2_000_000; // EOD batch: 2 million records

        // WRONG – starts at capacity 10, resizes ~40 times
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        List<Account> bad = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < expectedCount; i++) {
            bad.add(new Account("ACC" + i, "Holder" + i, BigDecimal.valueOf(i)));
        }
        long badTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        // CORRECT – pre-allocated, zero resizes
        start = System.nanoTime();
        List<Account> good = new ArrayList<>(expectedCount);
        for (int i = 0; i < expectedCount; i++) {
            good.add(new Account("ACC" + i, "Holder" + i, BigDecimal.valueOf(i)));
        }
        long goodTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        System.out.printf("Without pre-alloc: %d ms%n", badTime / 1_000_000);
        System.out.printf("With pre-alloc:    %d ms%n", goodTime / 1_000_000);
    }
}
```

```text
Without pre-alloc: 1250 ms
With pre-alloc:    890 ms
```

### What is the difference between size and capacity?

`size()` is the number of elements actually stored in the list. Capacity is the length of the internal array – the number of elements it can hold before resizing. Capacity is always greater than or equal to size.

There is no public `capacity()` method in ArrayList. You cannot directly inspect the capacity. But you can manage it:

- `ensureCapacity(minCapacity)` – guarantees the internal array has at least `minCapacity` slots. Useful before a known batch of additions.
- `trimToSize()` – shrinks the internal array to match `size()`. Useful after bulk loading to reclaim wasted memory.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;
import java.lang.reflect.Field;

public class SizeVsCapacityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ArrayList<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>();
        // After construction: size=0, capacity=0 (lazy)

        accounts.add("SAV2001");
        // After first add: size=1, capacity=10 (default allocated)

        for (int i = 0; i < 9; i++) accounts.add("ACC" + i);
        // size=10, capacity=10 (full)

        accounts.add("TRIGGER_RESIZE");
        // size=11, capacity=15 (grew 1.5x)

        System.out.println("Size: " + accounts.size());
        System.out.println("Capacity: " + getCapacity(accounts));

        // trimToSize – reclaim wasted memory
        accounts.trimToSize();
        System.out.println("After trimToSize - Capacity: " + getCapacity(accounts));
    }

    // Reflection to read internal capacity (for demonstration only)
    private static int getCapacity(ArrayList<?> list) throws Exception {
        Field f = ArrayList.class.getDeclaredField("elementData");
        f.setAccessible(true);
        return ((Object[]) f.get(list)).length;
    }
}
```

```text
Size: 11
Capacity: 15
After trimToSize - Capacity: 11
```

### What exceptions can occur?

- **IndexOutOfBoundsException**: `get(index)` or `set(index, e)` with `index < 0` or `index >= size()`.
- **ConcurrentModificationException**: modifying the list structurally (add/remove) while iterating with a for-each or iterator.
- **OutOfMemoryError**: when the internal array cannot be allocated (either the requested capacity exceeds `Integer.MAX_VALUE - 8`, or the JVM runs out of heap).

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class ArrayListExceptionsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001"));

        // IndexOutOfBoundsException
        try {
            accounts.get(5);
        } catch (IndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
            System.out.println("Index error: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // ConcurrentModificationException
        try {
            for (String acc : accounts) {
                if (acc.equals("SAV2001")) {
                    accounts.remove(acc);
                }
            }
        } catch (ConcurrentModificationException e) {
            System.out.println("Concurrent mod: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // Negative initial capacity
        try {
            new ArrayList<>(-1);
        } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
            System.out.println("Negative capacity: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Index error: Index 5 out of bounds for length 2
Concurrent mod: ConcurrentModificationException
Negative capacity: Illegal Capacity: -1
```

### What are common mistakes with ArrayList?

#### Mistake 1: Not setting initial capacity for large lists

```java
// WRONG – 40+ resizes for 1M elements
List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>();
for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) accounts.add("ACC" + i);

// CORRECT – zero resizes
List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>(1_000_000);
```

#### Mistake 2: Confusing size() with capacity

```java
ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(1000);
System.out.println(list.size()); // 0, NOT 1000!
// size() counts elements. Capacity is the array length.
// The list has 0 elements but space for 1000.
```

#### Mistake 3: Forgetting trimToSize after bulk load

```java
ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(1_000_000);
// Only 500 elements added (business logic changed)
for (int i = 0; i < 500; i++) list.add("ACC" + i);
// Internal array still has 1,000,000 slots – wasting ~4 MB!
list.trimToSize(); // now capacity = 500
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Lazy initialization.** Since Java 8, the default constructor creates an empty array `{}`, not a 10-element array. The capacity-10 allocation happens on the first `add()`. This saves memory when many ArrayLists are created but never populated (common in frameworks that pre-create beans).

- **System.arraycopy is a JVM intrinsic.** The array copying during resize and shifting is handled by `System.arraycopy()`, which the JVM replaces with optimized machine code (typically a single `memcpy` or `memmove` instruction). This is why ArrayList's O(n) shift is faster than you might expect.

- **Maximum capacity.** ArrayList's maximum capacity is `Integer.MAX_VALUE - 8` (2,147,483,639 elements). The `-8` headroom is for array header bytes on some VMs. In practice, you will hit `OutOfMemoryError` long before reaching this limit.

- **Amortized analysis.** Adding n elements to an empty ArrayList costs O(n) total, not O(n^2). Each element is copied at most O(log n) times across all resizes. This is the amortized O(1) guarantee.

- **Sublist views.** `ArrayList.subList()` returns a view backed by the parent. Structural changes to the parent invalidate the sublist. If you need a safe copy for pagination: `new ArrayList<>(list.subList(from, to))`.

- **Serialization.** ArrayList writes only the `size` elements during serialization (not empty slots). It has custom `writeObject`/`readObject` methods that skip the unused capacity.
